@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@cindex invocation
@cindex command line
The general form is:

@example
cgra [-d DEVICE] [-c FILE] COMMAND [ARGS...] [OUTPUT OPTIONS]
@end example

Options may appear anywhere on the line; the first non-option word is the
command. Every command checks its positional arity and allowed options before
loading configuration, opening output files or accessing a device. For example,
@code{ping unexpected} and @code{run add --cols 4} are errors. Device options
apply to device commands; @option{--cols} applies to @code{matvec} and
@code{emulate}.
@option{-h}/@option{--help} and @option{--version} print to standard output
and succeed without loading configuration. Diagnostics use standard error.

@code{cgra emulate --rows 2 --cols 3 --protocol 2} serves a rectangular v2
device on a pseudo-terminal (defaults: 4x4, v3; at most 16 PEs). SIGINT and
SIGTERM stop the service cleanly, including while output is backpressured.
The clockless model does not simulate the RTL parser watchdog.

@section Device resolution
@cindex device resolution
@cindex -d
The device is chosen, in order of precedence:

@enumerate
@item the @option{-d}/@option{--device} argument;
@item the @env{CGRA_DEVICE} environment variable;
@item the @code{default} device profile.
@end enumerate

@noindent
The resolved name may be a @dfn{profile} defined in a @file{.cgra} file, or a
literal: a serial path (anything containing @samp{/}, e.g.@:
@file{/dev/ttyUSB1}), the emulator @samp{sim:} (or @samp{sim:flaky} to force
the retry path), or @samp{auto}---the port saved by @command{cgra probe}.  A
profile's port and baud can be overridden by @env{CGRA_PORT} and
@env{CGRA_BAUD} (@pxref{Environment}).

@section Input vectors
@cindex input vectors
@cindex SRC
Commands that consume vectors accept a @dfn{source} (@var{SRC}) in three forms:

@table @asis
@item inline
a quoted list, @code{"1 2 3 4"} (whitespace- or comma-separated; @code{0x}/@code{0}
prefixes give hex/octal). Values must fit -32768..65535; malformed tokens and
out-of-range values are rejected.
@item @code{@@file}
read the numbers from @file{file}.
@item @code{-}
read from standard input.
@end table

@noindent
Sources are given with @option{--a} and @option{--b} (and @option{-m}/@option{--x}
for matrix commands).  If @option{--a} is omitted, positional numbers are used;
if there are none, the vector is read from standard input---so
@code{seq 0 255 | cgra run relu -d sim} works.

Positional input and @option{--a} are mutually exclusive. Matrix/convolution
aliases (@option{-m}/@option{--a}, @option{--x}/@option{--b}) cannot both be
specified for the same input. Unary modes reject an unused @option{--b}.
Put bare negative positional values after @code{--}, for example
@code{cgra run relu -d sim -- -3 -1 0 5}.

@section Interactive shell

@code{cgra shell} accepts commands without the leading @code{cgra}.
Single and double quotes group a vector into one argument. Unclosed quotes,
more than 126 command arguments, tokens exceeding 32768 bytes, or lines
exceeding 65536 bytes reject the entire line. The fallback without readline
has the same limits and drains an oversized line before reading another;
no truncated prefix is executed. Embedded NUL is rejected in stream input.
An inherited @option{-c} consumes two argument slots. A per-line config option
overrides the inherited one. @code{help} prints to stdout; @code{quit} exits.

@section Output formatting
@cindex output formatting
@cindex --io
@cindex --json
Vector commands (@code{dump}, @code{run}, @code{pipe}, @code{matvec},
@code{scan}, @code{conv}) accept the formatting options below. Benchmarks
accept @option{--json}; their JSON contains a benchmark report rather than
a vector. Other commands reject formatting options.

@table @code
@item --io PROFILE
use a named @code{[io]} profile (format @code{dec}/@code{hex}/@code{bin}, and a
separator).  The default profile is @code{dec}.
@item --json
emit @code{@{"count": N, "result": [@dots{}]@}}, so @code{... | jq '.result'}
composes.
@item --dtype u16|s16
interpret results as unsigned or signed 16-bit (default signed).
@item -o FILE
@itemx --out FILE
write to @file{FILE} instead of standard output. Output is staged until the
command succeeds, allowing the same file (or a hard link) as input and output.
Failed commands preserve the previous destination, except completed benchmark
reports, which are published even when they contain failures. Write errors cause a
nonzero exit status; the final copy is not atomic on filesystem failure.
@end table

File output is supported for vector commands, @code{bench}/@code{benchall},
@code{devices}, @code{modes}, @code{pipelines}, @code{io}, @code{config},
@code{check}, @code{ping} and @code{show}. Operational and interactive commands
(@code{init}, @code{reset}, @code{probe}, @code{emulate}, @code{selftest},
@code{shell}) reject @option{--out} before executing. Diagnostics go to standard
error, including validation failures from @code{check}.
